{"name":"Pot of Gold","hex":"#f6cd23","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pot of gold","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A life-changing achievement or fulfillment; happiness.","A sudden, financial windfall."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pot_of_gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pot of Gold (Mars)","blocks":[{"text":"Pot of Gold is the nickname for a knobby, softball-sized rock in Gusev Crater on Mars. During an examination by the Mars Exploration Rover Spirit on June 25, 2004, hematite was first detected by Spirit, suggesting a watery past on Mars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Pot_of_gold_upclose.jpg/330px-Pot_of_gold_upclose.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pot_of_Gold_(Mars)"},{"title":"Leprechaun","blocks":[{"text":"A leprechaun is a diminutive supernatural being in Irish folklore, classed by some as a type of solitary fairy. They are usually depicted as little bearded men, wearing a coat and hat, who partake in mischief. In later times, they have been depicted as shoe-makers who have a hidden pot of gold at the end of the rainbow."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Leprechaun_ill_artlibre_jnl.png/330px-Leprechaun_ill_artlibre_jnl.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leprechaun"},{"title":"Pot of Gold (The Game song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Pot of Gold\" is a song by American rapper The Game featuring American singer Chris Brown, released on June 28, 2011, as the second single from Game's fourth studio album The R.E.D. Album. The artists wrote the song alongside Sam Hook and producers The Futuristics."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/25/Pot_of_Gold_%28Game_song%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pot_of_Gold_(The_Game_song)"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Leprechaun_engraving_1900.jpg/330px-Leprechaun_engraving_1900.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Leprechaun_engraving_1900.jpg/330px-Leprechaun_engraving_1900.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A leprechaun counts his gold in this engraving c. 1900","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeprechaun_engraving_1900.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Menwithleprechaun.jpg/500px-Menwithleprechaun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Menwithleprechaun.jpg/500px-Menwithleprechaun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tourists with a novelty oversized Leprechaun in Dublin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMenwithleprechaun.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Beowulf_-_gold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Beowulf_-_gold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"The word gold in the Beowulf manuscript","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeowulf_-_gold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg/500px-Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg/1280px-Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg/500px-Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 5 mm (0.20 in) gold nugget (dot in front of frame) can be hammered into a gold foil of about 0.5 m2 (5.4 sq ft) in area.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASmall_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Gold_bullion_ap_001.JPG/500px-Gold_bullion_ap_001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Gold_bullion_ap_001.JPG/1280px-Gold_bullion_ap_001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Gold_bullion_ap_001.JPG/500px-Gold_bullion_ap_001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gold bars, also called ingots or bullion","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGold_bullion_ap_001.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Gold%28III%29_chloride_solution.jpg/500px-Gold%28III%29_chloride_solution.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Gold%28III%29_chloride_solution.jpg/500px-Gold%28III%29_chloride_solution.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gold(III) chloride solution in water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGold(III)_chloride_solution.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Vredefort_crater_cross_section_2.png/500px-Vredefort_crater_cross_section_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Vredefort_crater_cross_section_2.png/1280px-Vredefort_crater_cross_section_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Vredefort_crater_cross_section_2.png/500px-Vredefort_crater_cross_section_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Schematic of a NE (left) to SW (right) cross-section through the 2.020-billion-year-old Vredefort impact structure in South Africa and how it distorted the contemporary geological structures. The present erosion level is shown. Johannesburg is located where the Witwatersrand Basin (the yellow layer) is exposed at the \"present surface\" line, just inside the crater rim, on the left. Not to scale.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVredefort_crater_cross_section_2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Gold_nugget_%28Australia%29_4_%2816848647509%29.jpg/500px-Gold_nugget_%28Australia%29_4_%2816848647509%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campa